;;; A theory built on a theory, imported by an entry that does not name the
;;; lower one's — M1f S1f.6.25's witness of E-20 in P1f.6's `findings.md`,
;;; and since M1f S1f.6.35 the witness that nothing is left behind.
;;;
;;; `upper_without_entry.ein` imports `lower` by its entry and uses it; its
;;; own entry, `upper`, names its two rules and not `lower`. Until S1f.6.35 a
;;; selection kept the listed names and their closure, so `(upper)` brought
;;; `lower`'s declarations and none of its rules: `(ask a)` was derived and
;;; never answered, the goal had no row, and the loader said so in a `note:`
;;; line on every command.
;;;
;;; Since S1f.6.35 every import brings its module whole, with the module's own
;;; imports (the user, 2026-11-14: *"in all cases we import everything, the
;;; difference only in names"*): `upper_without_entry`'s own import of `lower`
;;; is an import of the program, so the lower theory is here and answers, as
;;; it does for `01_nested_import_whole.ein`, and a selection only names. The
;;; program lists what it writes, `wanted` and `got`. Today's whole import,
;;; `(import upper_without_entry :as u)`, gave the same answer before S1f.6.35
;;; (the stage's record, *Import invariance*). It states no hypothesis, to
;;; keep the answer small: R {∅}, and `a`'s double is `aa`.

(import upper_without_entry :symbols (upper wanted got))

(wanted a)

(solve :hypothesis-relations () :expect (region (model)))
(query :goal (got ?x ?y)
       :goal-text "{?x}'s double is {?y}"
       :expect (model (got a aa)))
